-- `VLT0115` — Rust's **adjacent** method-call syntax, and that is now all it is.
--
-- Plan 57 leaf 3 retired four spellings, because `.` was field access and
-- nothing else and the three spaced forms were an application over an
-- `Expr::Field`. Row 99 wave 3 takes doc 87 §1.2's other branch: `x.f y` means
-- `f(x, y)`, so the three spaced forms are now the surface and this fixture is
-- down to the one that is still not it.
--
-- What survives the inversion is `s.len(1)` — Rust's own spelling, with no
-- space before the `(`. It stays refused because Voltaic's application is
-- juxtaposition: `s.len 1`, like `f x`, with no parentheses and no commas. A
-- parenthesised argument list is the one shape that would make `.` mean two
-- different things depending on whitespace.
--
-- Plan 79's adjacency test is what makes the refusal narrow enough to keep:
-- refusing *any* following `(` also refused `.contains p.name (&text)`, where
-- the `(` opens the enclosing application's next argument rather than a call's
-- parameter list.

main : ()
main =
  s = String::new ()
  d = s.len(1)
  println! \-> "#{d}"
  ()
